WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.986
A real PQC migration program starts with cryptographic inventory.

00:00:03.986 --> 00:00:10.031
Organizations need to know where public-key cryptography is used before they can prioritize change.

00:00:10.031 --> 00:00:13.917
Inventory should include external services, internal services, certificates, VPNs,

00:00:13.917 --> 00:00:18.235
code signing, firmware signing, identity systems, third-party products, embedded devices,

00:00:18.235 --> 00:00:22.553
cloud services, APIs, build pipelines, and long-lived data protection workflows.

00:00:22.553 --> 00:00:27.302
Cryptographic agility is the ability to change algorithms, parameters, libraries, keys,

00:00:27.302 --> 00:00:30.325
and protocols without redesigning the entire system.

00:00:30.325 --> 00:00:33.347
Agility is not only a software feature.

00:00:33.347 --> 00:00:38.097
It is also procurement language, vendor management, architecture discipline, test coverage,

00:00:38.097 --> 00:00:41.119
documentation, incident response planning, and operational ownership.

00:00:41.119 --> 00:00:48.028
A system that cannot change cryptography safely will struggle with PQC and with future cryptographic transitions.

00:00:48.028 --> 00:00:51.914
Library and protocol support are necessary but not sufficient.

00:00:51.914 --> 00:00:55.800
HSM and KMS readiness, certificate authority support, validation expectations,

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:59.254
interoperability testing, logging visibility, deployment sequencing, rollback planning,

00:00:59.254 --> 00:01:01.413
and compliance requirements all matter.

00:01:01.413 --> 00:01:04.435
Hybrid transition strategies may help manage compatibility,

00:01:04.435 --> 00:01:11.344
but they still require careful testing and clear assumptions about what protection the hybrid design provides.

00:01:11.344 --> 00:01:15.662
Standards become useful when they are translated into engineering roadmaps.

00:01:15.662 --> 00:01:19.979
A roadmap should identify priority systems, owners, dependencies, vendor commitments,

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:23.002
test environments, rollout stages, and decision points.

00:01:23.002 --> 00:01:28.183
The goal is not to declare that the organization is quantum-safe overnight.

00:01:28.183 --> 00:01:34.660
The goal is to reduce uncertainty and build a path from inventory to tested deployment.
